Progress status for this period: regressing. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your strength dipped this week — a little recovery focus will get you back on track.",
  "summary": "This was a week where the body asked for a breather. You put in solid work — 28 sets and over 10,700 kg of total tonnage is genuinely respectable for a 72 kg athlete — but the strength numbers tell a story of accumulated fatigue rather than fresh progress. The good news is that a dip like this is a normal part of any serious training cycle, and addressing it now will set you up for a strong comeback next week. Your muscle group coverage remains broad and balanced, which is a real positive to carry forward.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "Strength is classified as regressing this period, and the index numbers back that up clearly. Chest dropped from 106 to 101 (a 5-point fall), back fell from 104 to 100 (4 points), and legs declined from 108 to 102 (6 points) — all three exceed the 3-point regression threshold. Shoulders, biceps, and triceps held flat at 103, 102, and 104 respectively, which is neutral but not progress. The pattern of drops concentrated in the largest, most demanding compound-lift muscle groups — chest, back, and legs — is a classic sign of systemic fatigue rather than a technique or programming flaw. No muscle group advanced this period, so there is no progressive overload to report. The priority now is restoring readiness so these same lifts can push to new highs in the coming period.",
    "status": "regressing"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps volume sits at just 4% of total weekly volume (3 sets), which is notably lower than triceps at 8.4% (3 sets). While triceps benefit from overlap with chest pressing work, biceps rely more heavily on direct training. For a muscle-building goal this imbalance is worth monitoring, though it is mild at this stage."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders account for only 10.4% of volume (4 sets), the lowest among the major upper-body groups. Given that chest and back each receive 6 sets, shoulders may benefit from one additional set to improve proportional stimulus over time."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "Three major muscle groups — chest, back, and legs — each regressed by 4 to 6 strength index points in a single period, which is a strong signal of accumulated fatigue. A brief deload, reducing load or volume by roughly 40–50% for one period while maintaining movement patterns, will allow the nervous system and muscles to recover fully. This is not a step backward; it is the mechanism that allows you to come back and actually exceed your previous strength levels.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Take a deload period by keeping the same exercises but reducing working weights by around 40–50% and dropping to 2–3 sets per muscle group — focus on clean technique and feeling the movement rather than pushing intensity.",
    "Prioritise sleep and nutrition during the deload: aim for at least 7–8 hours of sleep per night and ensure you are eating enough protein (around 1.6–2.0 g per kg of bodyweight, so roughly 115–144 g per day at your current weight) to support muscle repair.",
    "Add one dedicated set for biceps in your next full-effort period to bring it closer in line with triceps volume, supporting balanced arm development for your muscle-building goal.",
    "After the deload, return to your compound lifts — squat, deadlift or row, and press — with the intention of matching or exceeding your previous strength index peaks (chest 106, back 104, legs 108) as your short-term targets."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Significant strength regression across chest, back, and legs (drops of 4–6 index points each) with flat arms and shoulders — a deload was recommended to restore readiness before resuming progressive overload toward muscle-building goals."
}
